{# exportarr sidecar for the *arr stack — sonarr, radarr, prowlarr.

   One macro rather than three near-identical container blocks, for the reason the compose
   templates share `traefik.yml.j2` and `resources.yml.j2`: three copies drift, and the drift
   is invisible until one of them is the only app without a metric someone needs.

   WHY A SIDECAR, not a Deployment of its own. exportarr reaches its *arr over
   `http://localhost:<port>` inside the shared pod network namespace. As a separate workload it
   would need a ClusterIP hop, an entry in that *arr's NetworkPolicy caller list, and a fourth
   thing to keep in step. In-pod it needs none of those — and it is admitted to Prometheus for
   free, because the netpol baseline grants prometheus every pod carrying
   `netpol-baseline: enforced` on ANY port (no `ports:` key on that rule).

   `image` is a PARAMETER, not a literal. `test_no_literal_image_lines_in_k8s_templates` fails a
   hardcoded image in a k8s template, and `test_every_k8s_role_image_is_renovate_tracked` needs
   the pin to sit in a role's own `defaults/main.yml` as an `_image:` key — a shared var in
   group_vars is one directory short of the manager's file pattern and ages silently, which is
   exactly how `crowdsec_k8s_image` escaped tracking.

   Invoke at the container-list indent, e.g.:

       {{ exportarr('sonarr', sonarr_exportarr_image, container_item.port) }}
#}
{% macro exportarr(app, image, app_port, metrics_port=9707) -%}
- name: exportarr
          image: {{ image }}
          # The subcommand selects the API dialect — sonarr/radarr/prowlarr speak different
          # versions (v3/v3/v1) and expose different collectors.
          args: ["{{ app }}"]
          ports:
            - name: metrics
              containerPort: {{ metrics_port }}
          securityContext:
            allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
            runAsNonRoot: true
            runAsUser: {{ puid }}
            runAsGroup: {{ pgid }}
            readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
            capabilities:
              drop:
                - ALL
          env:
            - name: PORT
              value: "{{ metrics_port }}"
            # localhost, not the Service name: same pod, same network namespace. This is also
            # what keeps the sidecar out of the *arr's NetworkPolicy caller list — traffic that
            # never leaves the pod is not ingress.
            - name: URL
              value: "http://localhost:{{ app_port }}"
            # secretKeyRef, not a literal: the pod spec then carries a reference, so
            # `kubectl get pod -o yaml` and `describe pod` never show the key.
            - name: APIKEY
              valueFrom:
                secretKeyRef:
                  name: {{ app }}-exportarr
                  key: api_key
            - name: TZ
              value: "{{ tz }}"
          livenessProbe:
            httpGet:
              path: /healthz
              port: {{ metrics_port }}
            initialDelaySeconds: 30
            periodSeconds: 30
          # A readinessProbe would be actively harmful here. The *arr and this sidecar share one
          # pod, so a not-ready sidecar takes the whole pod out of its Service — an exporter
          # outage would become an application outage, which inverts the point of monitoring.
          resources:
            requests:
              cpu: 5m
              memory: 24Mi
            limits:
              memory: 64Mi
{%- endmacro %}
